Q: MongoDb .NET to replace string in array using ElemMatch() A: <p><code>elemMatch</code> is not needed here. You can use the <a href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/update/positional/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>$</code> positional update operator</a> to update that array value.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The positional <a href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/update/positional/#mongodb-update-up.-" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>$</code></a> operator acts as a placeholder for the <strong>first match</strong> of the update <a href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/query-documents/#std-label-read-operations-query-document" rel="nofollow noreferrer">query document.</a></p>
</blockquote>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>db.collection.update(
  {
    _id: &quot;000000000000000000001002&quot;,
    MyStringArray: &quot;round&quot;
  },
  {
    $set: { &quot;MyStringArray.$&quot;: &quot;triangle&quot; }
  }
)
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://mongoplayground.net/p/e7b0O25P_YV" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Mongo Playground</a></p>
<p>In .NET code, it would probably be:</p>
<pre><code>var idFilter = Builders&lt;Sample&gt;.Filter.Eq(d =&gt; d.Id, &quot;000000000000000000001002&quot;);
var elemFilter = Builders&lt;Sample&gt;.Filter.Eq(x =&gt; x.MyStringArray, &quot;round&quot;);
// may need AnyEq instead of Eq above
var filtersAnded = Builders&lt;Sample&gt;.Filter.And(idFilter, elemFilter);
var update = Builders&lt;Sample&gt;.Update.Set(&quot;MyStringArray.$&quot;, &quot;triangle&quot;);
UpdateResult result = collection.UpdateOne(filtersAnded, update);
</code></pre>
